E WA YAFNAA KULLO AHADIN \nby a great excellence for our Lord who remains while everyone perishes. \nوَاللهُ اَکْبَرُ تَسْبِیْحًا لَا یُحْصٰی وَ لَا یُدْرٰی \nWAL LAAHO AKBARO TASBEEHAN LAA YOHSAA WA LAA YUDRAA \nAllah is the Greatest with a glorification that cannot be numbered, nor perceived, \nوَ لَا یُنْسٰی وَ لَا یَبْلٰی وَ لَا یَفْنٰی وَ لَیْسَ لَہٗ مُنْتَہٰی \nWA LAA YUNSAA WA LAA YABLAA WA LAA YAFNAA WA LAYSA LAHU MUNTAHAA \nnor forgotten, nor worn out, nor exhausted, and for which there is no end. \nوَاللهُ اَکْبَرُ تَسْبِیْحًا یَدُوْمُ بِدَوَامِہٖ \nWAL LAAHO AKBARO TASBEEHAN YADOOMO BE-DAWAAMEHI \nAllah is the Greatest with a glorification that lasts with His everlastingness \nوَ یَبْقٰی بِبَقَآئِہٖ فِیْ سِنِی الْعَالَمِیْنَ \nWA YABQAA BE-BAQAAA-EHI FEE SENIL A’ALAMEENA \nand remains with His subsistence throughout the years of the worlds, \nوَ شُهُوْرِ الدُّهُوْرِ وَ اَیَّامِ الدُّنْیَا وَ سَاعَاتِ اللَّیْلِ وَالنَّہَارِ. \nWA SHOHOORID DOHOORE WA AYYAAMID DUNYAA WA SAA-A’ATIL LAYLE WAN NAHAARE. \nthe months of the ages, the days of the world, and the hours of the night and the day. \nوَاللهُ اَکْبَرُ اَبَدَا الْاَبَدِ وَ مَعَ الْاَبَدِ \nWAL LAAHO AKBARO ABADAL ABADE WA MA-A’L ABADE \nAllah is the Greatest forever and ever, \nمِمَّا لَا یُحْصِیْهِ الْعَدَدُ وَ لَا یُفْنِیْهِ الْاَمَدُ وَ لَا یَقْطَعُہُ الْاَبَدُ \nMIMMAA LAA YOHSEEHIL A’DADO WA LAA YUFNEEHIL AMADO WA LAA YAQTA-O’HUL ABADO \nand alongside eternity—a glorification that numbers cannot count, that time cannot exhaust, and that eternity cannot terminate. \nوَ تَبَارَكَ اللهُ اَحْسَنُ الْخَالِقِیْنَ.\nWA TABAARAKAL LAAHO AHSANUL KHAALEQEEN.\nAnd Blessed be Allah, the Best of Creators.\nYou may then say the famous supplicatory prayer, which begins with the following statement: ['Allaahumma Man Ta-a'bba-a'](AC10)\nThis supplicatory prayer has been previously mentioned within the rites at Friday nights.\nYou may then say the supplicatory prayer that has been mentioned by Shaykh al-Tusi in Misbah al-Mutahajjid. It is ascribed to Imam Zayn al-Aabedin (a.s.) and it begins with the following statement ‘allaahumma antal laaho rabbul a’alameen’ and is mentioned in the book ‘Mazaar al-Kabeer, p. 445’.\nIt is worth mentioning that this supplicatory prayer, which has not been cited herein because of its lengthiness, is also advisably said during the ritual of al-wuqoof al-Arafah (Halting At Mount Arafah), which is one of the rituals of Hajj.\nIt is also highly advisable to say the forty-seventh supplication of Saheefah al-Kaamelah al-Sajjaadiyyah. It is recommended to say the supplicatory prayer, which comprises all the requirements of worldly and religious life, with reverence.\nMany supplicatory prayers and acts of worship are reported to be done on this day for those who have the honor of being present on Mount Arafah. However, the best deed on this day is to pray Almighty Allah, since the most distinctive feature of the Arafah Day is praying. It is thus advisable to pray Him for the good of one’s brethren and sisters, the alive and the dead. The narration that has reported the manner of Abdullah ibn Jundab - may Allah have mercy upon him - during the ritual of halting on Mount Arafah when he prayed Almighty Allah for the good of his brethren-in-faith is too famous to be denied. Similarly,  Zayd al-Narsi has reported that Mu’awiyah ibn Wahab - the dignified, trustworthy scholar - prayed Almighty Allah for the good of his brethren-in-faith in names during the ritual of the halting at Mount Arafah on the Arafah Day. He then reported that Imam al-Sadiq (a.s.) mentioned a great merit for anyone who prays for the good of his brethren-in-faith on Mount Arafah on the ninth of Zilhajj. In fact, this narration is worth ponderation and consideration.\nSeizing this opportunity, I do please my faithful brethren to imitate such great personalities and thus pray for the good of their brethren-in-faith before they pray for themselves and to include me with their brethren-in-faith. I thus please you not to forget me in your prayers in my life and after my death since my face has been blackened by my sins.\nIt is also recommended to say the third comprehensive Ziyarah ([Ziyaarah al-Jaame’ah](AC13)).\nAt the last hour of the Arafah Day, it is recommended to say the following:\nيَا رَبِّ اِنَّ ذُنُوْبِيْ لَا تَضُرُّكَ \nYAA RABBE INNA ZONOOBEE LAA TAZURROKA\nO my Lord, indeed my sins do not harm You, \nوَ اِنَّ مَغْفِرَتَكَ لِيْ لَا تَنْقُصُكَ \nWA INNA MAGHFERATAKA LEE LAA TANQOSOKA\nand indeed Your forgiveness of me does not diminish You. \nفَاَعْطِنِيْ مَا لَا يَنْقُصُكَ \nFA-A-A’TENEE MAA LAA YANQOSOKA\nSo grant me that which does not diminish You, \nوَ اغْفِرْ لِيْ مَا لَا يَضُرُّكَ\nWAGH FIR LEE MAA LAA YAZURROKA\nand forgive me for that which does not harm You.\nIt is also recommended to say the following:\nاَللّٰهُمَّ لَا تَحْرِمْنِيْ خَيْرَ مَا عِنْدَكَ \nALLAAHUMMA LAA TAHRIMNEE KHAYRA MAA I’NDAKA\nO Allah, do not deprive me of the good that is with You \nلِشَرِّ مَا عِنْدِيْ \nLE-SHARRE MAA I’NDEE\nbecause of the evil that is with me. \nفَاِنْ اَنْتَ لَمْ تَرْحَمْنِيْ بِتَعَبِيْ وَ نَصَبِيْ \nFA-IN ANTA LAM TAHRIMNEE BE-TA-A’BEE WA NASABEE\nSo if You do not have mercy upon me for my fatigue and my toil, \nفَلَا تَحْرِمْنِيْۤ اَجْرَ الْمُصَابِ عَلٰى مُصِيْبَتِهٖ\nFA LAA TAHRIMNEE AJRAL MOSAABE A’LAA MOSEEBATEHI\nthen do not deprive me of the reward of the afflicted one for his affliction.\nWithin the supplicatory prayers on the Arafah Day, Sayyid Ibn Tawus has advised that immediately before sunset, one may say Dua al-Asharaat that has been previously cited. Accordingly, it is important not to neglect Dua al-Asharaat at the end of the Arafah Day since it is recommended to say this supplicatory prayer each morning and evening.\nIt is worth mentioning that the supplicatory prayers that are mentioned by al-Kaf’ami, as well as Sayyid Ibn Taaoos, are the same as the ones with which Dua al-Asharaat ends.",
  "merits": "Although it is not included with the feasts of Islam, the ninth of Zilhajj is one of the great feast days in Islam. On this day, Almighty Allah commanded His servants to obey and worship Him. He thus extended for them the tables of His benevolence and magnanimity. On this day too, Satan becomes abject, lowly, expelled, and angry more than any other day.\nIt is narrated that Imam Zayn al-Aabedin (a.s.), on the ninth of Zilhajj, heard someone begging people for financial aid. The Imam (a.s.) said to him, “Woe is you! On such a day, do you beg other than Almighty Allah? On this day, even fetuses in wombs are expected to be included with the mercy of Almighty Allah and they will be delighted.",
